The Truth About Love
I’m writing about love—the truth about love—not about relationship advice or cultural tropes. I am on a quest to define the nature of love, the thing-in-itself, the noumenon, rather than the phenomenon — the feeling. I want to venture into the epistemological territory of love’s true essence with the same reverence and awe that scientists, philosophers, mathematicians, and psychologists have historically applied to the study of consciousness.
I live twenty-five minutes away from one of world’s largest libraries. It stores 210 million printed and digital titles on every topic under the sun. When I started researching love, I envisioned finding the deeper answers to the mystery of love within weeks. What I found, however, was hundreds of thousands of titles, most of which fell squarely within three categories: romance, self-help, and social sciences.
In contrast, topics like “consciousness” and “intelligence” continually receive interest and contributions from neuroscientists, mathematicians, physicists, ecologists, psychologists, philosophers, and scientists across multiple domains of knowledge. But Love? Love has barely been revisited academically since interest in attachment theories peaked in 1950s, followed by a resurgence in terms like trauma bond, attachment styles — borrowed from therapy straight into pop-psychology. 
I plan to change that. The Truth About Love is my book-in-progress, my life’s work, and the purpose for this Substack.
In this article, however, I’ll cover a more timely topic: self-love.
I propose that the nature of self-love is not a feeling, but a drive to expand our Self model, in a continuous state of syntropy with the world. And so, understanding what self-love truly is becomes critical to a healthy inner life, lived to the highest standard.
Let’s dive in.
To love well is to live to the highest standard  
I spent my childhood on my grandparents’ farm and my adult life in the concrete jungle of one of the largest tech cities in the world. The quality of my life has changed; one may say it has improved, even. I can see why: everything I need I can access and everything I desire I could have. My younger self would lose her mind over the magnificent, magical Christmas tree they put up in Leicester Square every year. Yet, as a grown adult, I often sobbed uncontrollably at the sight of it, feeling utterly alone and disconnected in a crowd of strangers.
A high standard of living is such a complex, soul, body-and-matter kind of thing. It requires wisdom far more than wealth. Fast fashion is everywhere, but taste is hard to find. Devices stream all day, but watching stuff non-stop keeps your actual life offline. In today’s world of plenty, living life to the highest standard requires a whole new level of discernment.
Our inner life is no different.
World power means nothing. Only the unsayable, jewelled inner life matters - Rumi
The stuff you keep inside decides the quality of your life, for a mind persuaded of the wrong ideas is far more destructive to the Self than a really bad diet. And whilst a beautiful mind is often associated with culture and intellect, it’s the ineffable stuff like love and purpose that … you in such resplendent ways. Which is why, knowing to discern all that glitters from the soul’s most valuable jewels is essential to attaining a high standard of inner life. 
Self-love is one of those precious things that can single handedly toss you into a pit of misery or set you up with the “it” factor: charisma, confidence, and unapologetically being “you”. No wonder there’s a billion dollar industry selling all sorts of hacks, tricks, and secrets to enhance self-love. This is the world of all that glitters, the plenty that never fills you up. And it’s more dangerous today, where the crisis of meaning and the poverty of our inner lives are directly connected to consumption. And now, with the explosion of technology, an even greater danger has made itself indispensable to us and has become the infrastructure of our inner life. And that technology is AI.
Saving self-love from under the siege of AI
The forum internum, our inner life, was always meant to be protected. It is the only space where we have complete personal autonomy over feelings, thinking, fantasies, memories, beliefs, and anything that shapes our Self-narrative.
This is not the case anymore. In the name of personalization, enjoyment, and optimal interaction with our devices, the forum internum is now under siege from a constant stream of algorithmically curated information. And so is everything we associate with the Self, including self-love.
International human rights lawyer Susie Alegre argues that “the line between our inner and outer worlds is increasingly blurred by technology that makes inferences about our thoughts and emotions, such as emotion recognition technology, or technology that is designed to change how we think, feel and, ultimately, behave…”
Consider emotional AI, a class of technology used by Affectiva, TikTok and Meta. These tools gather biometric markers of our inner life non-consensually, to infer and predict emotions. Anything from arousal and interest to doubt, anger, sadness and vulnerability is harnessed to maximize engagement and consumption.
“Technology that is designed to read human beings’ inner lives and to change them raises serious concerns about the effective protection of the forum internum — and this technology is everywhere”, adds Susie Alegre.
But let’s be clear: the technology itself is not the culprit. The responsibility lies with human actors. It’s with people who have the power to influence corporate agendas and product roadmaps; people who draft T&Cs such that they frame emotional monitoring as a benefit to the consumer; engineers who develop technologies that connect disparate points to recreate our mental states just because it’s fun; creators who churn out whatever content the algorithms favor, and the influencers, gurus, and sellers whose profits depend entirely on convincing you that you do not love yourself enough.
To understand the nature of self-love is not an idealistic pursuit; it’s an act of discernement required to protect one’s forum internum, to prevent our cognitive and emotional integrity from being eroded by the tools and the content we inevitably interact with.
We know that AI is not directly chipping away at self-love. Rather, AI technologies are used to amplify highly emotionally charged, “viral” self-love advice that has nothing to stand on; AI is used to assess reactions and accurately target people when they’re at their lowest, and yet ready to make impulse purchases or passively give into doomscrolling. As users, we externalize emotional regulation and collude, rather than soothe, with repetitive content loops. AI actively shapes our feeds to reflect whatever advertisers and platforms deem the most engaging self-care meme or sellable digital service of the moment. Caught in the grip of this highly sophisticated marketing apparatus, where neurotechnology converges with artificial intelligence, self-love has devolved into little more than a programmatic lie.
The consequences reach far deeper than hiking your credit card bill with yet another exclusive coaching membership, or wasting your evenings on content loops about NLP hacks to reach your "highest potential."
People start to offload emotional processing to the content and digital products they consume. Inner dialogue is taken over by memes and trends. Digital life is accelerated, it replaces the development of authentic personal attitudes and insights based on a deeply felt and lived inner life, internal meaning-making is replaced by external discourse and consensus, by memeified attitudes, and even by opinions generated by chatGPT.
It is obvious, then, that the fundamental conditions to developing authentic, unique, personally fulfilling and aligned experiences are undermined. And when this is happening, can we truly believe we are nurturing self-love? Or something else? 
Therefore, it is imperative that we understand the truth about self-love. What it is, how it works, independent of external influences and most importantly, independent of influences on our inner life against which we have little power to fend ourselves with. When we understand the truth about self love, we will (hopefully) be less likely to buy into products, services, and any digital content that claims to have any substantial contribution to our self-narrative, and implicitly, the love we have towards ourselves.
What is the nature of self-love?
Self-love is not a feeling. It’s the Self in a syntropic state with the rest of the world.
Emotions are effemeral, like musical notes. They weave into moods and life-long passions. Primary emotions intertwine with powerful drives, the way pleasure intertwines with anything from lust to the ceaseless pursuit of beauty. Primary emotions to have more direct neurological correlates and signatures. However, to place the love of self strictly in the domain of emotionality would be a reductionist  just like it would be overly reductionist in to conflate bubble baths and the self-care paraphernalia with the true love of self. 
One way to appreciate healthy self-love is to see what happens when it goes haywire under extreme circumstances. Self love that is suppressed by an exceedingly rational, controlled life; whilst self-love that grows without limits looks like aggression and narcissism.
The self model is the most consistent process of consciousness. Barring lesions to the brain that induce autobiographical memory loss, although the demeanour, the essence of the person endures. 
In order to understand the nature of self love we need to understand the nature of the self; not its psychodynamic development, not its manifestations as ego, persona, and unconsciousness, not its neural correlates, but its purpose and teleological architecture. 
For that, we benefit first from understanding that the Self is a life-critical simulation of our world, and of our relation to the world. What are the primary functions of the self? Internal representation of the world (both inner and outer) or meaning-making, integrity preservation (only if you perceive yourself as a separate and autonomous “I” can you then seek to keep your self image coherent and your body safe, and growth - felt as a personal purpose.
